WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.916
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:04.314 --> 00:00:06.730
RETO MEIER: Hi, I'm Reto Meier,
Google Developer Advocate.

00:00:06.730 --> 00:00:09.130
Joining me today is Dave
Renson, Director of Customer

00:00:09.130 --> 00:00:11.590
Reliability Engineering.

00:00:11.590 --> 00:00:14.320
So, Dave, we all
know that SREs are

00:00:14.320 --> 00:00:16.660
trained to imagine the
worst possible scenario

00:00:16.660 --> 00:00:17.500
and prepare for it.

00:00:17.500 --> 00:00:18.500
DAVE RENSON: Uh-huh.

00:00:18.500 --> 00:00:19.450
RETO MEIER: Are
there ever scenarios

00:00:19.450 --> 00:00:20.908
which you've come
across which have

00:00:20.908 --> 00:00:24.340
made you want to pinch yourself
from a waking nightmare?

00:00:24.340 --> 00:00:26.249
DAVE RENSON: So you
know, Reto, cats

00:00:26.249 --> 00:00:27.790
are the patron saint
of the internet.

00:00:27.790 --> 00:00:28.240
We all know this.

00:00:28.240 --> 00:00:29.573
It's why there's so many videos.

00:00:29.573 --> 00:00:31.914
But did you know that
sharks are the mortal enemy

00:00:31.914 --> 00:00:32.580
of the internet?

00:00:32.580 --> 00:00:34.210
They're like the grumpy old men.

00:00:34.210 --> 00:00:35.939
So we had this
fiber cable once--

00:00:35.939 --> 00:00:38.230
RETO MEIER: I'm going to
interrupt you there, actually,

00:00:38.230 --> 00:00:40.660
because if you tell
everyone the story now,

00:00:40.660 --> 00:00:42.885
they're not going to have
any reason to come to Next.

00:00:42.885 --> 00:00:45.490
DAVE RENSON: There are has lots
of reasons to come to Next.

00:00:45.490 --> 00:00:47.934
Great technical keynotes, and
other stories that frankly

00:00:47.934 --> 00:00:50.350
make the shark story look like
the least interesting thing

00:00:50.350 --> 00:00:51.430
you've ever heard.

00:00:51.430 --> 00:00:54.430
Join us at Next, and especially
join us at the 5th Nine,

00:00:54.430 --> 00:00:57.880
a lounge we have dedicated
for SRE and DevOps people

00:00:57.880 --> 00:01:00.850
to come hear all the wonderful,
wacky, wild, and just

00:01:00.850 --> 00:01:03.210
plain weird stories of what
it takes to run production

00:01:03.210 --> 00:01:04.432
systems at web scale.

00:01:04.432 --> 00:01:06.390
RETO MEIER: We look
forward to seeing you then.

00:01:06.390 --> 00:01:09.740
[MUSIC PLAYING]

